1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,851
Host: Welcome to KC Desk Notes. Today we are unpacking one fresh research report and the question it raises.

2
00:00:06,211 --> 00:00:11,506
Host: Welcome back to the show. Today we’re digging into a major shift in Fed policy expectations.

3
00:00:11,866 --> 00:00:18,739
Host: The latest report from Nomura now sees the Fed on hold indefinitely. EN_B, what’s the big picture here?

4
00:00:19,099 --> 00:00:26,715
Guest: That’s right. The report’s title, ‘Hawkward Pause, ’ really captures it. The analysts had previously forecast two rate cuts this year,

5
00:00:27,075 --> 00:00:31,301
Guest: in September and December. Now they expect no cuts at all for the foreseeable future.

6
00:00:31,661 --> 00:00:36,537
Host: So what changed their minds? Was it one big thing or a combination of factors?

7
00:00:36,897 --> 00:00:44,885
Guest: It’s a combination. The report points to three main drivers. First, policy-related pressure for rate cuts has eased. Second, inflation is proving sticky.

8
00:00:45,245 --> 00:00:51,143
Guest: And third, the economy is still growing strongly. Together, they make a strong case for a prolonged pause.

9
00:00:51,503 --> 00:01:00,419
Host: Let’s start with the policy-related side. The report mentions President Trump and incoming Fed Chair Kevin Warsh. How does that play into the call?

10
00:01:00,779 --> 00:01:05,098
Guest: Interestingly, the report says President Trump has appeared to back off from pushing for rate cuts.

11
00:01:05,458 --> 00:01:12,238
Guest: He’s quoted in Fortune saying you can’t really look at the figures until the war is over. And he reportedly said he’ll let Warsh do what he wants.

12
00:01:12,598 --> 00:01:14,734
Host: So the policy-related heat has diminished.

13
00:01:15,094 --> 00:01:18,577
Host: And what about Warsh himself? Wouldn’t he be inclined to cut?

14
00:01:18,937 --> 00:01:26,414
Guest: The report thinks he likely is still motivated to ease. But the key point is that support among other Fed officials for an easing bias has been waning.

15
00:01:26,774 --> 00:01:33,137
Guest: Even dovish members are no longer advocating for cuts. So Warsh would need to convince a majority, and that’s looking tough.

16
00:01:33,497 --> 00:01:39,441
Host: Let’s talk about the inflation picture. The report mentions some new price pressures. What’s driving that?

17
00:01:39,801 --> 00:01:47,324
Guest: Tariff inflation has started to fade, but new pressures have emerged. The report specifically cites the Iran war and a global shortage of memory chips.

18
00:01:47,684 --> 00:01:56,275
Guest: These are pushing up input costs. The S&P Manufacturing PMI input price index surged to 79. 5 in May, the largest monthly increase on record.

19
00:01:56,635 --> 00:02:00,861
Host: That’s a big jump. And on the growth side, the economy seems to be holding up well.

20
00:02:01,221 --> 00:02:10,974
Guest: Exactly. The report highlights robust investment and steady consumption. Manufacturing and business capex are accelerating. Their Q2 GDP tracking is at 2. 6%,

21
00:02:11,334 --> 00:02:16,303
Guest: and real final sales to private domestic purchasers are at 2. 9%. That’s not an economy that needs stimulus.

22
00:02:16,663 --> 00:02:25,347
Host: So with strong growth and sticky inflation, the Fed has little reason to cut. But what about the labor market? Is that also supporting the pause?

23
00:02:25,707 --> 00:02:33,045
Guest: Yes. The report says labor markets are stabilizing. ADP data shows private payrolls accelerating. And initial jobless claims are sideways,

24
00:02:33,405 --> 00:02:39,303
Guest: which is actually positive given the seasonal tendency for them to rise into summer. So no signs of weakness there.

25
00:02:39,663 --> 00:02:46,768
Host: That all sounds very clear. But is there anything in the report that’s still uncertain? Something that makes the full report worth reading?

26
00:02:47,128 --> 00:02:53,862
Guest: Definitely. The report doesn’t fully settle what happens if inflation surprises to the upside or if growth suddenly falters.

27
00:02:54,222 --> 00:03:01,141
Guest: It also leaves open how Warsh’s leadership might evolve. The full report has more detailed data and scenarios. For example,

28
00:03:01,501 --> 00:03:06,610
Host: it breaks down the April FOMC minutes and shows how support for easing has shifted.

29
00:03:06,970 --> 00:03:15,793
Host: Good point. So for listeners who want the full picture, the report is a must-read. EN_B, any final thought on what this means for the market debate?

30
00:03:16,153 --> 00:03:22,933
Guest: I’d say this report reinforces the idea that we’re in a new regime. The old playbook of cutting rates at the first sign of trouble may not apply.

31
00:03:23,293 --> 00:03:28,866
Guest: The Fed is comfortable holding steady, and the data supports that. It’s a fascinating time for monetary policy.

32
00:03:29,226 --> 00:03:34,799
Host: Great insights. We’ll leave it there for now. Thanks for walking us through the report. And to our audience,

33
00:03:35,159 --> 00:03:39,431
Host: feel free to join the community discussion on the full report. Until next time.
